Find the length of the altitude drawn to the hypotenuse of a right triangle with legs of lengths a = 2.35 cm and b = 5.71 cm.
The length of the altitude drawn to the hypotenuse of a right triangle can be found using the formula:

{eq}h = \frac{ab}{\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}}
{/eq}
where a and b are the lengths of the legs of the triangle and h is the length of the altitude.

Substituting the given values into the formula, we get:

{eq}h = \frac{(2.35)(5.71)}{\sqrt{(2.35)^2 + (5.71)^2}}
{/eq}
{eq}h = \frac{13.45}{\sqrt{6.0525 + 32.5841}}
{/eq}
{eq}h = \frac{13.45}{\sqrt{38.6366}}
{/eq}
{eq}h = \frac{13.45}{6.216}
{/eq}
{eq}h = 2.16 cm
{/eq}
Therefore, the length of the altitude drawn to the hypotenuse of the right triangle is 2.16 cm.

